hadiths[4]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1143","Chapter on the jizyah, 1183 - Ahmad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Masruq, from Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to Yemen and ordered him to take from every thirty cattle a tabi' or tabi'ah, and from every adult a dinar or its equivalent in Ma'afir garments."
"1144","1184 - Ibn al-Muqri' narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, who heard Bajalah say: I was a scribe for Jaz' ibn Mu'awiyah, and the letter of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) reached us a year before his death: Kill every sorcerer, separate every Magian related by blood from his kinswoman as in the Book of Allah, prepare food and lay the sword upon your thigh. They ate without humming and cast down a mule-load or two mule-loads of silver. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) had not taken jizyah from the Magians until Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) testified that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had taken it from the Magians of Hajar."
"1145","1185 - Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, who said: Hisham ibn Hakim (may Allah be pleased with him) entered upon Umayr al-Ansari in Syria, who was a governor for Umar (may Allah be pleased with him). He entered upon him and found with him people of the Nabataeans standing in the sun. He said: What is the matter with these? He said: I detained them for the jizyah. Hisham said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever torments people in this world, Allah will torment him in the Hereafter. So Umayr released them and let them go."
"1146","1186 - Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil and Ibn al-Tabba' narrated to us; they said: Jarir narrated to us, from Qabus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two religions - and Ibn al-Tabba' said: two qiblahs - cannot both be right in one town, and no jizyah is due upon a Muslim."
